<Project>
  <!--
    The LATE half of the package's build contribution. The CoreCLR pin itself is in the .props
    beside this file, because a default has to be set before the Android SDK sets its own; see the
    import-order note there (#172).

    What belongs here is the opposite kind of thing: a check that needs the FINAL answer. By the
    time this file is imported the workload has resolved _AndroidRuntime, so this can see what the
    app is actually going to ship.

    Why it is worth an error rather than a warning. A consumer who ends up on Mono has no way to
    find out except by shipping an APK that dies in OnCreate, on a device, with a managed exception
    whose stack points at innocent string code. That is the single most expensive failure this
    package can hand someone, and it is completely invisible at build time. Turning it into a build
    error that names the cause costs a consumer who genuinely wants Mono one property.
  -->
  <Target Name="CupriFaceCheckAndroidRuntime" BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(UseMonoRuntime)' == 'true' and '$(CupriFaceAllowMonoRuntime)' != 'true'">
    <Error Code="CUPRI0001"
           Text="CupriFace.Android requires the CoreCLR runtime, and this app is building against Mono (UseMonoRuntime=true, _AndroidRuntime=$(_AndroidRuntime)). The Mono runtime pack in .NET 10 (10.0.11) has a code-generation defect that crashes CupriFace apps during startup, in OnCreate, before the first frame — it reproduces on arm64 hardware and x64 emulators, under AOT and JIT alike. Remove UseMonoRuntime from this project to take the package's default, or set CupriFaceAllowMonoRuntime=true to build anyway and accept the crash. Forensics: samples/AndroidProbe/MONO-CRASH.md." />
  </Target>
</Project>
